# Database migrations required

Any change that alters durable PostgreSQL structure or migration-managed seed data must ship a matching pair under `deploy/migrations/`.

## When a migration is required

- Adding/dropping/renaming tables, columns, indexes, constraints, or enums
- Changing column types, defaults, or nullability that existing rows must honor
- Inserting or updating rows that migrations own (system accounts, built-in bots, catalog seeds)
- Renaming or splitting migration-managed data so fresh DBs and upgrades stay in sync

## When a migration is not required

- Pure Go/RPC/UI logic with no schema impact
- Temporary local scratch SQL under ignored `scripts/_*.sql` paths
- Docs-only or in-memory store changes

## How to write them

- Add both `YYYYMMDDHHMMSS_name.up.sql` and matching `.down.sql`
- Use the next timestamp after the latest file in `deploy/migrations/` (do not reuse or skip versions already recorded in `schema_migrations`)
- Prefer idempotent guards where safe (`IF NOT EXISTS` / `IF EXISTS`) for additive local forks
- Never delete a migration that may already be applied on a running database; restore missing files instead
- Keep Go `sqlc` queries/models in sync when columns change (or use explicit raw SQL until regenerated)

## Checklist before finishing

- [ ] `.up.sql` and `.down.sql` both exist for the new version
- [ ] Fresh migrate and upgrade-from-previous both make sense
- [ ] App code that reads/writes the new shape is updated in the same change
